  "text": "Solution 3 is the most likely to be correct, but I can't verify it from memory.\n\nSolutions 1 and 2 rest on heuristic exponent counting. Solution 1 applies the div-curl lemma to a pairing that doesn't give the sharp threshold for this inclusion. Solution 2 uses a Sobolev-embedding bookkeeping argument and a claimed \"classical threshold\" of $n^2/(n+1)$ for Hessian-determinant weak continuity, which I can't confirm and which doesn't clearly match the constraint structure here. Neither gives a convincing reason that the threshold is sharp.\n\nSolution 3 cites a specific published result, Breden, Lopes Filho and Nussenzveig Lopes on rigidity and flexibility of the steady Euler/pressureless equations in the plane, with critical exponent $p = 3/2$. I recall this as a real line of work with a $3/2$ threshold for the steady pressureless system, but I have not checked the paper's abstract or statement, and the citation itself could be imperfect. The $3/2$ value is also the one that fits the problem's form (rigid above $p+\\epsilon$, flexible below $p-\\epsilon$) in that literature.\n\nSo I'd select Solution 3, with moderate confidence. Checking the paper's abstract would settle it.\n\nCHOICE: 3",
